<title>Japan in Ten Days — November 2026</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --bg: #fbf9f6;
    --surface: #ffffff;
    --surface-2: #f4f0ea;
    --ink: #1c1a17;
    --ink-2: #4a453e;
    --ink-3: #7a736a;
    --rule: #e2dcd2;
    --accent: #a8321f;
    --accent-soft: #f6e7e3;
    --good: #2f6b4f;
    --good-soft: #e6f0ea;
    --warn: #8a5a12;
    --warn-soft: #f8eeda;
    --shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(28,26,23,.05), 0 8px 24px rgba(28,26,23,.05);
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) {
      --bg: #14130f;
      --surface: #1d1b17;
      --surface-2: #262320;
      --ink: #f2eee7;
      --ink-2: #c3bcb1;
      --ink-3: #8e867b;
      --rule: #33302a;
      --accent: #e8735a;
      --accent-soft: #33211d;
      --good: #7fc39d;
      --good-soft: #1b2a23;
      --warn: #d8ac63;
      --warn-soft: #2c2417;
      --shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3), 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,.28);
    }
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] {
    --bg: #14130f;
    --surface: #1d1b17;
    --surface-2: #262320;
    --ink: #f2eee7;
    --ink-2: #c3bcb1;
    --ink-3: #8e867b;
    --rule: #33302a;
    --accent: #e8735a;
    --accent-soft: #33211d;
    --good: #7fc39d;
    --good-soft: #1b2a23;
    --warn: #d8ac63;
    --warn-soft: #2c2417;
    --shadow: 0 1px 2px rgba(0,0,0,.3), 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,.28);
  }

  body {
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, "Helvetica Neue", sans-serif;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
  }
  .wrap { max-width: 50rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 1.25rem 6rem; }

  h1, h2, h3, h4 { line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 640; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 2.9rem); margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 3.6vw, 1.7rem);
    margin: 3.5rem 0 1rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 2rem 0 .6rem; }
  p { margin: 0 0 1rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 620; }
  a { color: var(--accent); }

  .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 40rem; }
  .kicker {
    font-size: .75rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); font-weight: 640; margin: 0 0 .75rem;
  }

  .facts {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1px; background: var(--rule); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 2rem 0 0;
  }
  .facts div { background: var(--surface); padding: .9rem 1rem; }
  .facts dt { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); margin-bottom: .15rem; }
  .facts dd { margin: 0; font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); }

  .card {
    background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 12px; padding: 1.25rem 1.35rem; margin: 1rem 0;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow);
  }
  .day { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: .6rem; margin-bottom: .15rem; }
  .day .n {
    font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft);
    padding: .18rem .5rem; border-radius: 999px; font-weight: 660;
  }
  .day .date { font-size: .85rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .day h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1.15rem; flex-basis: 100%; }

  .card ul { margin: .5rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.15rem; }
  .card li { margin-bottom: .45rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .card li::marker { color: var(--ink-3); }

  .note {
    margin-top: .9rem; padding: .7rem .9rem; border-radius: 8px;
    font-size: .92rem; background: var(--surface-2); color: var(--ink-2);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--ink-3);
  }
  .note.warn { background: var(--warn-soft); border-left-color: var(--warn); }
  .note.good { background: var(--good-soft); border-left-color: var(--good); }
  .note b { color: var(--ink); }

  .scroll { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 10px; background: var(--surface); }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: .93rem; min-width: 34rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: .6rem .85rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; }
  th { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .07em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); font-weight: 640; background: var(--surface-2); }
  td { color: var(--ink-2); }
  td.num, th.num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
  tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
  tr.total td { font-weight: 660; color: var(--ink); background: var(--surface-2); border-top: 2px solid var(--rule); }
  caption { caption-side: bottom; text-align: left; padding: .6rem .85rem; font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-3); }

  .cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
  .cols .card { margin: 0; }
  .cols h4 { margin: 0 0 .5rem; font-size: .95rem; }
  .cols ul { padding-left: 1.1rem; margin: 0; font-size: .93rem; }
  .cols li { margin-bottom: .35rem; }

  footer { margin-top: 4rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: .85rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  footer a { color: var(--ink-3); }
</style>

<div class="wrap">

<p class="kicker">Ten days · two people · moderate budget</p>
<h1>Japan, first time</h1>
<p class="lede">Tokyo → Hakone → Kyoto → Nara → Osaka. Nine nights on the ground, one long-haul flight in and a different one out, no rail pass, and roughly <strong>¥538,000 (~$3,430) for two</strong> before international airfare.</p>

<dl class="facts">
  <div><dt>Suggested dates</dt><dd>Fri 13 – Sun 22 Nov 2026</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Flights</dt><dd>Into Haneda, out of Kansai</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Rail pass</dt><dd>No — buy singles</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Rate used</dt><dd>¥157 = $1</dd></div>
</dl>

<h2>The shape of the trip, and why</h2>

<p><strong>Open-jaw flights.</strong> Fly into Tokyo Haneda and home out of Osaka Kansai. It usually costs the same as a round trip and saves you a wasted ¥28,000 backtrack and half a day. Book this as a single multi-city ticket, not two one-ways.</p>

<p><strong>Four cities, three bases.</strong> Tokyo (4 nights), Hakone (1 night), Kyoto (3 nights), Osaka (1 night). Nara happens as a stop on the road between Kyoto and Osaka rather than as a separate day trip — it sits almost exactly between them, so you see it with your luggage already forwarded ahead.</p>

<p><strong>Skip the Japan Rail Pass.</strong> This is the big money decision and the answer is clearly no. From 1 October 2026 the 7-day ordinary pass costs ¥53,000 per person. This itinerary's long-distance rail comes to about ¥14,000 per person. The pass made sense when it was ¥29,650; on a Tokyo–Kyoto–Osaka route in 2026 it is roughly a ¥78,000 mistake for two people. Buy individual tickets.</p>

<h3>About the dates</h3>
<p>November is the right month and also the most contested one. Two things pull against each other:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Kyoto's autumn colour peaks roughly 20 Nov – 7 Dec</strong>, later than most people assume, and long-range signals for 2026 point slightly later than average.</li>
  <li><strong>Peak colour means crushing crowds and hotel rates 30–40% above baseline.</strong> Kyoto room rates in late November are already running well above last year.</li>
</ul>
<p>The suggested window, <strong>13–22 November</strong>, deliberately puts you in Kyoto on 18–21 November: early peak, with the north and the higher ground (Arashiyama, Nanzen-ji, Eikan-dō) already turning, while the worst of the crush and the worst of the pricing arrives the week after. It also lands your Kyoto days on Wed/Thu/Fri rather than a weekend, which matters enormously at Kiyomizu-dera and Tōfuku-ji.</p>
<div class="note warn"><b>Two dates to avoid if you shift the window:</b> 3 November (Culture Day, a Tuesday) and 23 November (Labour Thanksgiving, a Monday, creating a 21–23 November long weekend). Domestic travel and hotel prices spike on both. Your last night, Sat 21 Nov in Osaka, clips the front of that long weekend — book it early and it's fine, since Osaka isn't a foliage destination.</div>
<div class="note"><b>The cheaper alternative:</b> shift to <b>6–15 November</b>. You trade peak Kyoto colour for noticeably lower hotel rates, thinner crowds everywhere, and better foliage in Hakone (which peaks earlier, at altitude). If budget matters more than maple leaves, take this window.</div>

<h2>Day by day</h2>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 1</span><span class="date">Fri 13 Nov · Tokyo</span><h3>Land, and don't be ambitious</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Haneda → city on the Keikyu line (¥300 pp to Shinagawa, ~15 min) or the Tokyo Monorail (¥520 pp to Hamamatsuchō). Both beat the ¥1,400 limousine bus unless you're wrecked.</li>
    <li>Set up an IC card <em>before</em> you leave the airport (see Practicalities below) — you'll want it for every turnstile from here on.</li>
    <li>Drop bags, then keep it to one neighbourhood. From a Shinjuku base: Omoide Yokochō and Golden Gai for the alley atmosphere, a bowl of ramen, and an early night.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note">Jet lag from North America or Europe hits hardest on nights 2 and 3, not night 1. Plan the easiest day for Day 3, not today.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 2</span><span class="date">Sat 14 Nov · Tokyo</span><h3>West side: Meiji, Harajuku, Shibuya</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meiji Jingū at opening (~6:15am).</strong> Enormous cedar-lined approach, essentially empty at dawn, ten minutes from Harajuku station. This is the single best use of your jet lag.</li>
    <li>Takeshita-dōri for the spectacle, then Omotesandō for the architecture and the department-store basement food halls.</li>
    <li>Shibuya Scramble Crossing, then <strong>Shibuya Sky</strong> for the sunset slot (~¥2,700 pp, timed entry, <strong>book a month out</strong> — golden-hour tickets go first).</li>
    <li>Dinner: izakaya in Shibuya or Ebisu.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note warn">It's a Saturday. Harajuku and Shibuya are at maximum density from about 1pm. Front-load the morning.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 3</span><span class="date">Sun 15 Nov · Tokyo</span><h3>East side: old Tokyo</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sensō-ji in Asakusa, early.</strong> The temple grounds are open 24 hours; Nakamise-dōri's shops open around 9. Being there at 7am is a completely different experience from being there at 11.</li>
    <li>Kappabashi kitchen-supply street — knives, ceramics, plastic food. One of the better shopping streets in Tokyo and almost free of tour groups.</li>
    <li>Sumida River walk toward Tokyo Skytree, or the Asahi building for the view back.</li>
    <li>Afternoon: Ueno Park and the <strong>Tokyo National Museum</strong> (¥1,000 pp) — the best single museum in the country for context on everything you'll see in Kyoto.</li>
    <li>Evening: Yanaka's low-rise lanes, or Akihabara if that's your thing.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 4</span><span class="date">Mon 16 Nov · Tokyo</span><h3>Market, digital art, the modern city</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tsukiji Outer Market</strong> for breakfast — tamagoyaki, uni, tuna skewers. Go at 8am; most stalls wind down by early afternoon. No booking, just turn up.</li>
    <li><strong>teamLab Borderless</strong> at Azabudai Hills (~¥3,800 pp, timed entry, <strong>book 3–4 weeks out</strong>). Take the earliest slot; the rooms are physically small and the experience collapses when crowded.</li>
    <li>Zōjō-ji and Tokyo Tower, or the Imperial Palace East Gardens (free) if you'd rather walk.</li>
    <li>Last Tokyo dinner: this is the night to spend on something good — a proper sushi counter, or yakitori in Ginza.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note"><b>Swap option:</b> if you'd rather have a day out of the city, <b>Kamakura</b> is 1 hour and ¥940 each way (Yokosuka line) — Great Buddha, Hase-dera, hiking trails between temples. <b>Nikkō</b> is grander but a full 2-hour commitment each way, and by mid-November its best foliage has already gone.</div>
  <div class="note warn"><b>Don't plan on the Ghibli Museum.</b> It is closed for renovation from 1 October 2026 until mid-April 2027.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 5</span><span class="date">Tue 17 Nov · Tokyo → Hakone</span><h3>Mountains, sulphur, and a proper onsen</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>First: forward your big bags to the Kyoto hotel</strong> via the front desk (takkyubin, ~¥2,300 per case, next-day delivery). Take an overnight bag to Hakone. This is the single best logistics move of the trip — the Hakone loop involves five changes of vehicle, several of them small.</li>
    <li><strong>Odakyu Romancecar, Shinjuku → Hakone-Yumoto</strong>, ~85 min. Reserve the seats (¥1,200 pp surcharge); the front-row observation seats sell out.</li>
    <li>The Hakone loop, counter-clockwise, all covered by the Freepass: Hakone Tozan switchback railway → Gōra → cable car → <strong>ropeway over Ōwakudani</strong> (volcanic vents, black eggs, the Fuji view if the weather plays) → pirate boat across Lake Ashi → the torii gate standing in the water at Hakone Shrine.</li>
    <li>Check into the ryokan by 5pm. Onsen before dinner, kaiseki dinner in yukata, onsen again after.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note good"><b>Fuji is visible roughly 50% of winter mornings and much less often in the afternoon.</b> If the mountain is out when you reach Ōwakudani, stop and look properly — don't assume tomorrow.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 6</span><span class="date">Wed 18 Nov · Hakone → Kyoto</span><h3>Sculpture, then the bullet train west</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Morning onsen, then the <strong>Hakone Open-Air Museum</strong> (¥1,600 pp) — Henry Moore and Picasso in a mountain valley, with a foot bath. Genuinely good, and 5 minutes from Gōra.</li>
    <li>Down to Odawara for a ~12:30 <strong>Hikari shinkansen to Kyoto</strong> (~2h05, ¥12,320 pp reserved). Note: Nozomi trains do not stop at Odawara — Hikari is the right train and only slightly slower on this leg.</li>
    <li>Arrive Kyoto ~14:45. Your forwarded luggage is waiting at the hotel.</li>
    <li>Evening: <strong>Nishiki Market</strong> before it closes (~6pm), then walk Pontochō alley and Gion's Hanamikōji at dusk, ending at Yasaka Shrine, which is lit and open late.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note warn">Buy a bentō and a beer at Odawara station before boarding. This is not a formality — eating an ekiben on the shinkansen is one of the small pleasures of Japan, and there is nothing to buy on board worth having.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 7</span><span class="date">Thu 19 Nov · Kyoto</span><h3>Eastern hills, from dawn</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kiyomizu-dera at 6:00am opening</strong> (¥500 pp). This is non-negotiable in foliage season. At 6am you have the veranda nearly to yourself; by 10am the approach streets are shoulder-to-shoulder.</li>
    <li>Walk down through <strong>Sannenzaka and Ninenzaka</strong> — preserved stone lanes, best before the shops open — then Kōdai-ji and Maruyama Park.</li>
    <li>Late morning: Chion-in's colossal gate, then break for lunch.</li>
    <li>Afternoon: <strong>Nanzen-ji</strong> (free grounds, brick aqueduct, ¥600 for the garden) → the <strong>Philosopher's Path</strong> → <strong>Ginkaku-ji</strong>, the Silver Pavilion (¥500 pp). This stretch is where Kyoto's maples are best in the third week of November.</li>
    <li>Evening: <strong>Eikan-dō illumination</strong> (¥600–1,000, mid-Nov to early Dec, no advance booking — but expect a queue).</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note">Kyoto's buses are the weak point of the city — slow, crowded, and cash-fiddly in November. Use the subway where it goes, taxis freely for short hops (¥800–1,500 gets you across most of the east side, and split two ways it beats the bus for time), and walk the rest.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 8</span><span class="date">Fri 20 Nov · Kyoto</span><h3>West and north, plus a shrine after dark</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Arashiyama bamboo grove at 7:30am.</strong> It is a short, narrow path and it is genuinely magical empty and genuinely miserable at midday. Go early or don't go.</li>
    <li><strong>Tenryū-ji</strong> garden (¥500 pp) — a 14th-century pond garden that borrows the mountain behind it — then Ōkōchi Sansō villa (¥1,000 pp, includes matcha, and half as crowded as everything around it).</li>
    <li>Optional: the <strong>Sagano Romantic Train</strong> along the gorge (¥880 pp) — <strong>book ahead</strong>, it sells out completely in foliage season.</li>
    <li>Afternoon: <strong>Kinkaku-ji</strong>, the Golden Pavilion (¥500 pp) — 30 minutes, one view, worth it — and <strong>Ryōan-ji</strong>'s rock garden (¥600 pp) nearby.</li>
    <li><strong>Evening: Fushimi Inari after dark.</strong> The shrine is open 24 hours, free, and lit. The torii tunnels at 8pm with almost no one in them are the best hour you will spend in Kyoto. Allow 2 hours for the full loop up the mountain, or 45 minutes for the lower gates.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 9</span><span class="date">Sat 21 Nov · Kyoto → Nara → Osaka</span><h3>Deer, a very large Buddha, and Osaka at night</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Forward your bags from the Kyoto hotel to the Osaka hotel before 10am (~¥1,600 per case) and travel light.</li>
    <li><strong>Kintetsu limited express, Kyoto → Nara</strong>, ~35 min, ¥1,160 pp. Arrives right at the edge of the park.</li>
    <li><strong>Tōdai-ji</strong> (¥800 pp) — the world's largest bronze Buddha in one of the world's largest wooden buildings; the scale does not photograph. Then the deer park, and <strong>Kasuga Taisha</strong>'s three thousand stone lanterns along a mossy forest approach.</li>
    <li>Lunch and a wander in <strong>Naramachi</strong>, the old merchant quarter.</li>
    <li>~4pm: <strong>Kintetsu Nara → Osaka-Namba</strong>, ~45 min, ¥680 pp.</li>
    <li>Evening: <strong>Dōtonbori</strong> for the neon, then eat properly — okonomiyaki, kushikatsu (don't double-dip the sauce), takoyaki. Shinsekai if you want the rougher, better version of the same thing.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note good">Nara's deer bow for crackers and will absolutely mug you for a paper map. Keep loose food out of open bags.</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="day"><span class="n">Day 10</span><span class="date">Sun 22 Nov · Osaka → home</span><h3>One last thing, then Kansai</h3></div>
  <ul>
    <li>Depending on your flight: <strong>Osaka Castle</strong> park (free grounds, ¥600 for the keep) or the <strong>Umeda Sky Building</strong> floating observatory (¥2,000 pp), or one last run at Kuromon Market for breakfast.</li>
    <li><strong>Nankai from Namba to Kansai Airport</strong> — 45 min, ¥970 pp on the airport express, ¥1,490 pp on the Rapi:t if you want reserved seats and a bit of theatre.</li>
    <li>Be at KIX <strong>3 hours</strong> before an international departure. It is a busy airport and the check-in queues for US-bound carriers are long.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note"><b>Tax-free shopping:</b> if you bought anything duty-free, the goods and receipts may be inspected at departure. Keep them accessible rather than buried in checked luggage.</div>
</div>

<h2>Getting between cities: what it costs</h2>

<div class="scroll">
<table>
  <caption>Prices are per person one-way unless noted, for two adults, at 2026 fares.</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Leg</th><th>How</th><th>Time</th><th class="num">Per person</th><th class="num">For two</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Haneda → central Tokyo</td><td>Keikyu line</td><td>15 min</td><td class="num">¥300</td><td class="num">¥600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Around Tokyo (4 days)</td><td>IC card, metro + JR</td><td>—</td><td class="num">¥4,000</td><td class="num">¥8,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Shinjuku → Hakone</td><td>Hakone Freepass 2-day + Romancecar seat</td><td>85 min</td><td class="num">¥8,300</td><td class="num">¥16,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Odawara → Kyoto</td><td>Hikari shinkansen, reserved</td><td>2 h 05</td><td class="num">¥12,320</td><td class="num">¥24,640</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Around Kyoto (3 days)</td><td>Subway, bus, some taxis</td><td>—</td><td class="num">¥3,000</td><td class="num">¥6,000</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Kyoto → Nara</td><td>Kintetsu limited express</td><td>35 min</td><td class="num">¥1,160</td><td class="num">¥2,320</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Nara → Osaka-Namba</td><td>Kintetsu express</td><td>45 min</td><td class="num">¥680</td><td class="num">¥1,360</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Around Osaka</td><td>Subway</td><td>—</td><td class="num">¥800</td><td class="num">¥1,600</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Namba → Kansai Airport</td><td>Nankai airport express</td><td>45 min</td><td class="num">¥970</td><td class="num">¥1,940</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Luggage forwarding ×2 legs</td><td>Yamato takkyubin, 2 cases each</td><td>next day</td><td class="num">—</td><td class="num">¥7,800</td></tr>
    <tr class="total"><td colspan="4">Ground transport, total</td><td class="num">¥70,860</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p><strong>On the Hakone Freepass:</strong> the ¥7,100 2-day version from Shinjuku includes the round trip to Odawara, and you'll only use the outbound half since you continue west. The alternative — a ¥5,000 Odawara-area-only pass plus separate Odakyu tickets out — comes to about the same money with more faff. Take the Shinjuku version.</p>

<p><strong>On booking shinkansen seats:</strong> reservations open exactly one month before departure. Use <a href="https://smart-ex.jp/en/">SmartEX</a> (Tokaido line, English, foreign cards accepted) or just buy at a ticket machine on the day — this route runs every few minutes and mid-week in November you will not be shut out. Reserve if you want to sit together with luggage; note that <strong>oversized bags (over 160cm total) require a free advance reservation</strong> for the space behind the last row, which is another reason to forward the big cases.</p>

<h2>Where to stay</h2>

<div class="cols">
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Tokyo — 4 nights</h4>
    <p style="font-size:.9rem;margin:0 0 .5rem"><strong>Shinjuku</strong> is the right first-timer base: the Odakyu line to Hakone leaves from here, and it's on the Yamanote loop.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Target ~¥22,000/night for a double.</li>
      <li>Look at business-hotel chains — Sotetsu Fresa Inn, Mitsui Garden, Hotel Gracery, Tokyu Stay. Small rooms, spotless, excellent value.</li>
      <li>Alternatives: <strong>Marunouchi/Tokyo Station</strong> (calmer, better for shinkansen) or <strong>Asakusa</strong> (cheaper, more atmospheric, less central).</li>
      <li>Avoid Roppongi as a base unless nightlife is the point.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Hakone — 1 night</h4>
    <p style="font-size:.9rem;margin:0 0 .5rem">This is the one deliberate splurge, and it should be a <strong>ryokan with an onsen and half board</strong>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Budget ~¥45,000 for two, dinner and breakfast included — which offsets a day of food costs.</li>
      <li>Stay in <strong>Gōra</strong> or <strong>Sengokuhara</strong> (better views, on the loop) rather than Hakone-Yumoto.</li>
      <li>If either of you has tattoos, filter for rooms with a <strong>private open-air bath</strong> (<em>kashikiri</em> or <em>rotenburo-tsuki</em>) — worth it regardless.</li>
      <li><strong>Book this first.</strong> Good ryokan sell out for November months ahead.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Kyoto — 3 nights</h4>
    <p style="font-size:.9rem;margin:0 0 .5rem">The tightest, most expensive booking of the trip. Aim for <strong>Kyoto Station area</strong> or <strong>Karasuma/Shijō</strong>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Target ~¥28,000/night — a 30–40% foliage-season premium on the usual ¥18–20,000.</li>
      <li>Karasuma/Shijō is the sweet spot: subway on both lines, walkable to Nishiki and Gion.</li>
      <li>Kyoto Station is less charming but unbeatable for the Nara and Osaka legs.</li>
      <li>Higashiyama is lovely and will cost half again as much.</li>
      <li><strong>Kyoto's lodging tax rose on 1 March 2026</strong> — at this room rate it's ¥400 per person per night, but it climbs steeply above ¥50,000/night.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Osaka — 1 night</h4>
    <p style="font-size:.9rem;margin:0 0 .5rem">One night, chosen for airport access and dinner. <strong>Namba</strong> or <strong>Shinsaibashi</strong>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Target ~¥18,000/night — Osaka runs meaningfully cheaper than Kyoto.</li>
      <li>Namba puts you a 5-minute walk from Dōtonbori and on the direct Nankai line to KIX.</li>
      <li>Umeda/Osaka Station also works and is quieter, but adds a transfer to the airport.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Book ahead vs. turn up</h2>

<p>The genuinely useful version of this list is short. Japan is mostly a turn-up country; the exceptions are specific and worth respecting.</p>

<div class="scroll">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Now — 3+ months</strong></td><td>Open-jaw flights; Hakone ryokan; all three city hotels</td><td>November is peak season. Kyoto in particular is more competitive than it has ever been, and good ryokan for late November go a year out.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>~1 month</strong></td><td>Shibuya Sky (sunset slot); teamLab Borderless; Sagano Romantic Train; any high-end sushi or kaiseki counter</td><td>All timed-entry or counter-seated with hard caps. Sunset slots and foliage-season train seats are the first to vanish.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>~1 month, optional</strong></td><td>Shinkansen reserved seats; Romancecar seats; Kintetsu limited express</td><td>Reservations open exactly 30 days out. Not strictly necessary mid-week, but cheap insurance if you're travelling with luggage.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>A few days out</strong></td><td>One or two nicer dinners per city</td><td>Small counters seat 8–12 people. Have your hotel concierge call — many places don't take online or foreign bookings.</td></tr>
    <tr class="total"><td colspan="3" style="font-weight:600">Just turn up</td></tr>
    <tr><td colspan="3">Every temple and shrine on this itinerary — Sensō-ji, Meiji Jingū, Kiyomizu-dera, Fushimi Inari, Kinkaku-ji, Ryōan-ji, Nanzen-ji, Ginkaku-ji, Tenryū-ji, Tōdai-ji, Kasuga Taisha. Nishiki Market, Kuromon Market, Tsukiji Outer Market. Dōtonbori and every restaurant on it. Ramen, izakaya, conveyor sushi, department-store food halls. Tokyo National Museum. Hakone Open-Air Museum. Osaka Castle. The Eikan-dō night illumination (queue, don't book). Nara's deer.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="note warn"><b>Two things people book and shouldn't:</b> the JR Pass (see above — it loses you money on this route), and guided day tours of Kyoto temples. Kyoto is trivially navigable on your own and the tours lock you into the worst hours of the day.</div>

