<h3>Where the money moves if you want it to</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Save ~$250:</strong> shift to the 6–15 November window and pay off-peak Kyoto rates.</li>
  <li><strong>Save ~$180:</strong> skip the ryokan and stay in a Hakone business hotel — but you'd be cutting the most memorable night of the trip.</li>
  <li><strong>Save ~$130:</strong> drop the Osaka night, return to Kyoto on Day 9 and take the Haruka express (75 min, ¥3,060 pp) to KIX on Day 10. Simpler, less food.</li>
  <li><strong>Spend ~$300 more:</strong> upgrade two dinners to serious sushi or kaiseki counters. In Japan this buys a disproportionate amount of memory.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Practicalities</h2>

<div class="cols">
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Money</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Japan is far more card-friendly than its reputation, but small restaurants, temples, shrines, and markets are <strong>cash only</strong>.</li>
      <li>Carry <strong>¥30,000–40,000</strong> and top up at <strong>7-Eleven ATMs</strong>, which reliably accept foreign cards. Bank ATMs often don't.</li>
      <li>Temple entry is typically ¥500–1,000 per person, cash, exact-ish change appreciated.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>IC card — do this on arrival</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>On <strong>iPhone</strong>: add <strong>Welcome Suica Mobile</strong> or Suica in Apple Wallet before you leave the airport. Tops up with a foreign card via Apple Pay; Mastercard and Amex are the most reliable, some foreign Visas fail.</li>
      <li>On <strong>Android</strong>: international handsets lack the FeliCa chip. Get a <strong>physical Welcome Suica</strong> at the JR East Travel Service Center in Haneda Terminal 3.</li>
      <li>Physical Welcome Suica expires after 28 days and the remaining balance is <strong>not refundable</strong> — don't over-load it.</li>
      <li>One card covers trains, buses, subways, lockers and convenience stores in every city on this route.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Connectivity</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>An <strong>eSIM</strong> is the cheapest and simplest option — around $15–25 each for 10 days, activated before you fly.</li>
      <li>A pocket wifi rental only makes sense if one of you has an incompatible phone.</li>
      <li><strong>Google Maps works properly in Japan</strong>, including live train platforms and carriage numbers. Trust it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>November weather</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Tokyo and Kyoto: roughly <strong>8–17°C</strong>, dry and clear — the best weather of the year.</li>
      <li>Hakone is 5–8°C colder and can be wet or fogged in. Pack a warm layer for the ropeway.</li>
      <li>Kyoto mornings are cold enough that a 6am temple visit needs gloves.</li>
      <li>Bring shoes you can slip off easily — you'll do it several times a day — and expect <strong>15,000–20,000 steps daily</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>A few things worth knowing</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No tipping.</strong> Anywhere, for anything. It causes confusion, not pleasure.</li>
  <li><strong>Don't eat while walking</strong>, and don't talk on the phone on trains.</li>
  <li>Rubbish bins are genuinely scarce. Carry a small bag for your own litter — everyone does.</li>
  <li><strong>Lunch is the value meal.</strong> Restaurants that charge ¥6,000 at dinner often do the same food as a ¥1,500 lunch set. Eat your best meals at midday and your cheap ones at night.</li>
  <li>Convenience-store food is genuinely good, not a compromise. Onigiri, egg sandwiches, and fried chicken from 7-Eleven or Lawson will carry a lot of your breakfasts.</li>
  <li><strong>Tattoos</strong> can bar you from public onsen. Check your ryokan's policy or book a room with a private bath.</li>
</ul>

